  "review_report": {
    "findings": "PROOF_ADEQUATE subject=sha256:ab24edb57620292039baec0e56f0122a37e64b44134271c07bc333879bde4011 aggregate_sha256=1f6487b0ebb6f073995489ac2ad83c05ea4ae5ecef9e13b3a7d61cd98b6d6977\n\nThe two 506-entry maps and shared tables resolve the inventory omission. They preserve row order and supply the paths, hashes, byte counts, categories, and origins needed to reconstruct the scanner inputs and sorted filesystem digest inputs. Each lane\u2019s classified `refusal/inputs/control/INPUT-FREEZE.json` is 1,388 bytes and its digest matches manifest pin 291 and the corresponding test literal. Both scanner records show exit 2 for the altered `DEV-PUBLIC-INPUT-FREEZE.json` digest and exit 0 for the exact inventory.\n\nThe aggregate supports all seven claims. The material witnesses show the refusal-capture and freeze-pin failures separately and together. The discovery control detects omission of both designated tests, and the policy controls exercise classifier integrity, epoch binding, and missing-receipt refusal. The manifest-bound refusal requires exit 1, empty stdout, and the exact stderr JSON error object, with retained stream validation preserved. It establishes an operational observation only. R0 credit remains zero.\n\nI reviewed the supplied records without tools or file changes and relied on the supplied machine-checked hashes. P14 supplies the comparison baseline, and both historical 36-of-38 prefixes remain incomplete and consumed. Before collection, the canonical gate must reject the stale receipt and altered witness and accept the exact receipt. New owner policies using the existing key and authority instance are required after source admission.",
